The bug and fixes, verified:

```js
// THE BUG — var: ONE binding for the whole loop
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 0);
}
// logs: 3, 3, 3 — all three closures share the SAME i,
// which is 3 by the time any timer fires

// FIX 1 — let: a FRESH binding per iteration (verified: 0 1 2)
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 0);
}
// each iteration gets its own i — closures capture their own

// FIX 2 — IIFE: pre-let, you created a new scope manually
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  (function (captured) {
    setTimeout(() => console.log(captured), 0);   // captured is a PARAMETER
  })(i);   // pass the CURRENT value — a copy per call
}

// FIX 3 — bind the value through a function factory
const makeTimer = (n) => () => console.log(n);
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) setTimeout(makeTimer(i), 0);
```

The mental model (the interview core): a closure captures the BINDING (the variable slot), not a snapshot of the value. With `var`, the loop has one binding; by timer-time it holds the final value. With `let`, the spec creates a NEW binding per iteration — each closure gets its own slot, and the trap can't happen. This is also WHY closures power counters/partial application correctly — they're sharing state deliberately; the loop bug is that sharing applied where you wanted per-iteration snapshots.

The modern React angle (where this still bites): a map over items creating deferred callbacks (onClick handlers with item ids) — with let/const in the map param, each closure captures its own item. But the analogous bug appears with stale STATE: a handler created on render 1 reads render-1's state — that's the same 'capture at creation' semantics applied to React state, fixed with functional updates or refs instead of let.

```javascript
function makeCounter() {
  let count = 0;
  return () => ++count;
}
const next = makeCounter();
next(); // 1
next(); // 2
```

